WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:01.576 --> 00:05.626 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference May 7,

00:05.656 --> 00:07.076 align:middle
2025 CHAIR POWELL.

00:07.076 --> 00:08.066 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:08.066 --> 00:10.066 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:10.066 --> 00:13.096 align:middle
on achieving our dual-mandate
goals of maximum employment

00:13.096 --> 00:15.796 align:middle
and stable prices for the
benefit of the American people.

00:17.116 --> 00:18.456 align:middle
Despite heightened uncertainty,

00:18.596 --> 00:20.806 align:middle
the economy is still
in a solid position.

00:20.906 --> 00:25.346 align:middle
The unemployment rate remains
low, and the labor market is at

00:25.346 --> 00:26.746 align:middle
or near maximum employment.

00:27.816 --> 00:29.466 align:middle
Inflation has come
down a great deal

00:29.546 --> 00:30.546 align:middle
but has been running somewhat

00:30.546 --> 00:33.086 align:middle
above our 2 percent
longer-run objective.

00:36.066 --> 00:37.056 align:middle
In support of our goals,

00:37.056 --> 00:38.956 align:middle
today the Federal Open
Market Committee decided

00:38.956 --> 00:41.496 align:middle
to leave our policy
interest rate unchanged.

00:42.666 --> 00:45.656 align:middle
The risks of higher unemployment
and higher inflation appear

00:45.656 --> 00:48.476 align:middle
to have risen, and we believe
that the current stance

00:48.476 --> 00:52.086 align:middle
of monetary policy leaves us
well positioned to respond

00:52.086 --> 00:55.106 align:middle
in a timely way to potential
economic developments.

00:55.866 --> 00:58.566 align:middle
I will have more to say
about monetary policy

00:58.566 --> 01:00.976 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

01:01.746 --> 01:02.646 align:middle
[Cough] Pardon me.

01:03.606 --> 01:07.956 align:middle
Following growth of 2.5 percent
last year, GDP was reported

01:07.956 --> 01:11.226 align:middle
to have edged down in the first
quarter, reflecting swings

01:11.226 --> 01:14.596 align:middle
in net exports that were likely
driven by businesses bringing

01:14.596 --> 01:16.856 align:middle
in imports ahead of
potential tariffs.

01:17.686 --> 01:21.586 align:middle
This unusual swing complicated
GDP measurement last quarter.

01:22.626 --> 01:24.216 align:middle
Private domestic
final purchases,

01:24.216 --> 01:28.376 align:middle
or PDFP-which excludes net
exports, inventory investment,

01:28.376 --> 01:31.546 align:middle
and government spending-grew
at a solid 3 percent rate

01:31.546 --> 01:34.146 align:middle
in the first quarter, the
same as last year's pace.

01:35.126 --> 01:38.396 align:middle
Within PDFP, growth of consumer
spending moderated while

01:38.396 --> 01:40.626 align:middle
investment in equipment
and intangibles rebounded

01:40.626 --> 01:41.946 align:middle
from weakness in
the fourth quarter.

01:43.416 --> 01:45.556 align:middle
Surveys of households
and businesses, however,

01:45.556 --> 01:47.396 align:middle
report a sharp decline
in sentiment

01:47.446 --> 01:50.116 align:middle
and elevated uncertainty
about the economic outlook,

01:50.596 --> 01:53.206 align:middle
largely reflecting
trade policy concerns.

01:53.756 --> 01:57.686 align:middle
It remains to be seen how these
developments might affect future

01:57.686 --> 01:58.796 align:middle
spending and investment.

02:00.576 --> 02:03.226 align:middle
In the labor market,
conditions have remained solid.

02:03.936 --> 02:07.336 align:middle
Payroll job gains
averaged 155,000 per month

02:07.586 --> 02:08.716 align:middle
over the past three months.

02:09.316 --> 02:12.136 align:middle
The unemployment rate, at
4.2 percent, remains low

02:12.136 --> 02:14.606 align:middle
and has stayed in a narrow
range for the past year.

02:15.846 --> 02:17.706 align:middle
Wage growth has continued
to moderate,

02:17.886 --> 02:19.516 align:middle
while still outpacing inflation.

02:20.596 --> 02:22.926 align:middle
Overall, a wide set
of indicators suggests

02:22.926 --> 02:25.786 align:middle
that conditions in the labor
market are broadly in balance

02:26.136 --> 02:27.876 align:middle
and consistent with
maximum employment.

02:29.266 --> 02:30.846 align:middle
The labor market is not a source

02:30.906 --> 02:33.036 align:middle
of significant inflationary
pressures.

02:34.626 --> 02:36.986 align:middle
Inflation has eased
significantly from its highs

02:36.986 --> 02:40.836 align:middle
in mid-2022 but remains
somewhat elevated relative

02:40.836 --> 02:42.706 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal.

02:44.006 --> 02:46.646 align:middle
Total PCE prices
rose 2.3 percent

02:46.646 --> 02:48.336 align:middle
over the 12 months
ending in March;

02:49.106 --> 02:51.516 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

02:51.576 --> 02:54.116 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
2.6 percent.

02:55.526 --> 02:58.206 align:middle
Near-term measures of inflation
expectations have moved up,

02:58.526 --> 03:01.196 align:middle
as reflected in both market-
and survey-based measures.

03:02.286 --> 03:04.786 align:middle
Survey respondents, including
consumers, businesses,

03:04.786 --> 03:07.476 align:middle
and professional
forecasters, point to tariffs

03:07.506 --> 03:08.496 align:middle
as the driving factor.

03:10.056 --> 03:12.606 align:middle
Beyond the next year or
so, however, most measures

03:12.606 --> 03:15.036 align:middle
of longer-term expectations
remain consistent

03:15.406 --> 03:17.066 align:middle
with our 2 percent
inflation goal.

03:18.816 --> 03:22.696 align:middle
Our monetary policy actions
are guided by our dual mandate

03:22.696 --> 03:25.046 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

03:25.046 --> 03:26.126 align:middle
for the American people.

03:26.966 --> 03:28.616 align:middle
At today's meeting,
the Committee decided

03:28.616 --> 03:32.166 align:middle
to maintain the target range for
the federal funds rate at 4¼

03:32.666 --> 03:35.476 align:middle
to 4½ percent and to
continue reducing the size

03:35.476 --> 03:36.216 align:middle
of the balance sheet.

03:37.696 --> 03:39.486 align:middle
The new Administration
is in the process

03:39.486 --> 03:41.716 align:middle
of implementing substantial
policy changes

03:41.786 --> 03:45.026 align:middle
in four distinct areas:
trade, immigration,

03:45.116 --> 03:46.776 align:middle
fiscal policy, and regulation.

03:47.966 --> 03:49.326 align:middle
The tariff increases announced

03:49.326 --> 03:52.216 align:middle
so far have been significantly
larger than anticipated.

03:52.816 --> 03:55.066 align:middle
All of these policies are
still evolving, however,

03:55.476 --> 03:58.216 align:middle
and their effects on the
economy remain highly uncertain.

03:59.076 --> 04:02.366 align:middle
As economic conditions
evolve, we'll continue

04:02.366 --> 04:05.406 align:middle
to determine the appropriate
stance of monetary policy based

04:05.406 --> 04:07.826 align:middle
on the incoming data,
the outlook,

04:07.826 --> 04:08.876 align:middle
and the balance of risks.

04:10.076 --> 04:12.056 align:middle
If the large increases
in tariffs

04:12.156 --> 04:15.346 align:middle
that have been announced are
sustained, they are likely

04:15.446 --> 04:18.796 align:middle
to generate a rise in inflation,
a slowdown in economic growth,

04:19.136 --> 04:20.556 align:middle
and an increase in unemployment.

04:21.296 --> 04:23.286 align:middle
The effects on inflation
could be short lived,

04:23.856 --> 04:25.956 align:middle
reflecting a one-time
shift in the price level.

04:26.046 --> 04:29.566 align:middle
It is also possible that the
inflationary effects could

04:29.566 --> 04:30.886 align:middle
instead be more persistent.

04:31.566 --> 04:33.646 align:middle
Avoiding that outcome
will depend on the size

04:33.646 --> 04:36.556 align:middle
of the tariffs' effect-tariff
effects, on how long it takes

04:36.556 --> 04:39.536 align:middle
for them to pass through fully
into prices, and, ultimately,

04:39.536 --> 04:42.716 align:middle
on keeping longer-term inflation
expectations well anchored.

04:43.906 --> 04:46.016 align:middle
Our obligation is to keep
longer-term inflation

04:46.016 --> 04:48.826 align:middle
expectations well anchored and
to prevent a one-time increase

04:48.926 --> 04:52.076 align:middle
in the price level from becoming
an ongoing inflation problem.

04:53.246 --> 04:55.006 align:middle
As we act to meet
that obligation,

04:55.006 --> 04:56.736 align:middle
we'll balance our
maximum-employment

04:56.736 --> 04:59.696 align:middle
and price-stability mandates,
keeping in mind that,

04:59.696 --> 05:02.546 align:middle
without price stability, we
cannot achieve the long periods

05:02.546 --> 05:04.136 align:middle
of strong labor market
conditions

05:04.416 --> 05:05.996 align:middle
that benefit all Americans.

05:07.456 --> 05:09.746 align:middle
We may find ourselves in
the challenging scenario

05:09.746 --> 05:12.036 align:middle
in which our dual-mandate
goals are in tension.

05:12.476 --> 05:15.966 align:middle
If that were to occur, we would
consider how far the economy is

05:15.966 --> 05:18.596 align:middle
from each goal, and the
potentially different time

05:18.596 --> 05:21.466 align:middle
horizons over which those
respective gaps would be

05:21.466 --> 05:22.676 align:middle
anticipated to close.

05:23.586 --> 05:26.396 align:middle
For the time being, we're
well positioned to wait

05:26.396 --> 05:28.966 align:middle
for greater clarity before
considering any adjustments

05:28.966 --> 05:30.096 align:middle
to our policy stance.

05:31.726 --> 05:34.076 align:middle
At this meeting, the Committee
continued its discussions

05:34.076 --> 05:35.576 align:middle
as part of our five-year review

05:35.576 --> 05:37.206 align:middle
of our monetary policy
framework.

05:38.106 --> 05:40.976 align:middle
We focused on inflation
dynamics and the implications

05:40.976 --> 05:43.026 align:middle
for our monetary
policy strategy.

05:44.026 --> 05:45.446 align:middle
Our review includes outreach

05:45.446 --> 05:48.046 align:middle
and public events involving
a wide range of parties,

05:48.486 --> 05:50.756 align:middle
including Fed Listens
events around the country

05:50.956 --> 05:52.946 align:middle
and a research conference
next week.

05:54.326 --> 05:56.466 align:middle
Throughout this process,
we're open to new ideas

05:56.466 --> 05:59.296 align:middle
and critical feedback, and
we will take on board lessons

05:59.296 --> 06:01.866 align:middle
of the last five years in
determining our findings.

06:02.616 --> 06:04.866 align:middle
We intend to wrap up the
review by late summer.

06:06.616 --> 06:08.176 align:middle
The Fed has been
assigned two goals

06:08.176 --> 06:11.346 align:middle
for monetary policy-maximum
employment and stable prices.

06:11.906 --> 06:14.416 align:middle
We remain committed to
supporting maximum employment,

06:14.826 --> 06:17.306 align:middle
bringing inflation sustainably
to our 2 percent goal,

06:17.866 --> 06:20.646 align:middle
and keeping longer-term
inflation expectations

06:20.706 --> 06:21.326 align:middle
well anchored.

06:22.216 --> 06:23.336 align:middle
Our success in delivering

06:23.336 --> 06:25.586 align:middle
on these goals matters
to all Americans.

06:26.296 --> 06:29.256 align:middle
We understand that our actions
affect communities, families,

06:29.256 --> 06:30.696 align:middle
and businesses across
the country.

06:31.506 --> 06:33.926 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

06:35.096 --> 06:36.806 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

06:36.886 --> 06:38.486 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

06:38.486 --> 06:39.646 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

06:40.346 --> 06:40.726 align:middle
Thank you.

06:40.936 --> 06:42.306 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

06:42.816 --> 06:43.276 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

06:43.906 --> 06:45.756 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

06:46.146 --> 06:46.996 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

06:46.996 --> 06:49.046 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chair,
for taking my question.

06:49.706 --> 06:52.426 align:middle
A lot has happened
since the last meeting.

06:52.426 --> 06:54.286 align:middle
There have been tariffs
put on, tariffs taken off,

06:54.286 --> 06:56.826 align:middle
and meanwhile, there was a
bill advancing in Congress.

06:56.826 --> 06:58.606 align:middle
And I just wonder
if I could press you

06:58.606 --> 07:00.506 align:middle
on the last part
of your statement.

07:00.506 --> 07:04.106 align:middle
Are you any closer now
to deciding which side

07:04.106 --> 07:07.536 align:middle
of the mandate is going
to need urgent care first?

07:09.206 --> 07:10.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:10.476 --> 07:15.306 align:middle
Well, so, as we noted in our-in
our statement-postmeeting

07:15.306 --> 07:17.986 align:middle
statement, we've judged that
the risks to higher employment

07:17.986 --> 07:19.656 align:middle
and higher inflation
have both risen.

07:19.656 --> 07:22.966 align:middle
And this, by the way, of
course, is compared to March.

07:23.366 --> 07:24.636 align:middle
So that's what we can say.

07:25.136 --> 07:27.726 align:middle
I don't think we
can say, you know,

07:27.786 --> 07:29.976 align:middle
which way this will shake out.

07:29.976 --> 07:33.316 align:middle
I think there's a great deal of
uncertainty about, for example,

07:33.316 --> 07:37.276 align:middle
where tariff policies are going
to, to settle out and also,

07:37.276 --> 07:39.346 align:middle
when they do settle out,
what will be the implications

07:39.346 --> 07:41.386 align:middle
for the economy for
growth and for employment.

07:41.386 --> 07:42.776 align:middle
I think it's too
early to know that.

07:43.386 --> 07:46.056 align:middle
So, I mean, ultimately, we
think our policy rate is in-is

07:46.086 --> 07:50.416 align:middle
in a good place to stay as
we await further clarity

07:50.416 --> 07:52.286 align:middle
on tariffs and, ultimately,

07:52.286 --> 07:53.516 align:middle
their implications
for the economy.

07:53.796 --> 07:54.276 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

07:54.276 --> 07:57.226 align:middle
Hearing you describe what
you're looking for in terms

07:57.226 --> 07:58.366 align:middle
of being able to
make a decision,

07:59.036 --> 08:02.296 align:middle
it sounded like that's a
long-term process before you

08:02.296 --> 08:03.736 align:middle
sound like you're going
to be comfortable,

08:03.736 --> 08:05.496 align:middle
or the Committee
would be comfortable,

08:05.496 --> 08:07.836 align:middle
to act on what the
data's telling you.

08:07.836 --> 08:08.426 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:08.716 --> 08:09.726 align:middle
I don't think we know.

08:09.726 --> 08:12.496 align:middle
You know, I think-look
where we are today.

08:12.956 --> 08:16.036 align:middle
We have an economy that, if
you "look through," you know,

08:16.856 --> 08:20.556 align:middle
the sort of distortions in Q1
GDP, you've still got an economy

08:20.556 --> 08:22.226 align:middle
that looks like it's
growing at a solid pace.

08:22.226 --> 08:23.746 align:middle
The labor market
appears to be solid.

08:24.396 --> 08:27.636 align:middle
Inflation is running just
a bit above 2 percent.

08:27.636 --> 08:30.596 align:middle
So it's an economy that's been
resilient and is in good shape,

08:31.366 --> 08:33.456 align:middle
and our policy is
sort of modestly

08:33.456 --> 08:35.336 align:middle
or moderately restrictive.

08:35.386 --> 08:37.466 align:middle
It's 100 basis points
less restrictive

08:37.466 --> 08:39.406 align:middle
than it was last fall.

08:39.886 --> 08:42.676 align:middle
And so we think that leaves us
in a good place to wait and see.

08:42.676 --> 08:44.256 align:middle
We don't think we
need to be in a hurry.

08:44.256 --> 08:45.496 align:middle
We think we can be patient.

08:45.636 --> 08:46.926 align:middle
We're going to be
watching the data.

08:47.646 --> 08:50.776 align:middle
The data may move quickly or
slowly, but we do think we're

08:50.776 --> 08:53.826 align:middle
in a good position, where we
are, to, to let things evolve

08:53.886 --> 08:56.866 align:middle
and become clearer in terms

08:56.866 --> 08:59.116 align:middle
of what should be the
monetary policy response.

09:00.386 --> 09:03.056 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:03.256 --> 09:04.656 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

09:04.656 --> 09:06.426 align:middle
Nick Timiraos for the
Wall Street Journal.

09:06.426 --> 09:08.176 align:middle
Chair Powell, there's
naturally a lot of [inaudible]

09:08.176 --> 09:11.496 align:middle
around 2021 in supply shocks.

09:12.176 --> 09:13.296 align:middle
But there are some who argue

09:13.296 --> 09:15.576 align:middle
that the current situation
has notable differences.

09:15.576 --> 09:16.806 align:middle
Energy costs are down.

09:17.296 --> 09:20.506 align:middle
Housing imbalances look nothing
like they did four years ago.

09:20.836 --> 09:22.916 align:middle
Labor demand appears to
be gradually cooling,

09:22.956 --> 09:25.026 align:middle
with wage growth
running below 4 percent.

09:26.006 --> 09:27.496 align:middle
What do you see right now

09:27.896 --> 09:31.746 align:middle
that could nourish higher
inflation beyond a rise

09:31.746 --> 09:33.236 align:middle
in goods prices this year?

09:33.236 --> 09:35.126 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:35.126 --> 09:38.896 align:middle
I think the underlying
inflation picture is, is good.

09:38.896 --> 09:41.936 align:middle
It's what you see, which is
inflation now running a bit

09:41.936 --> 09:42.846 align:middle
above 2 percent.

09:42.846 --> 09:47.146 align:middle
And we've had basically decent
readings in housing services

09:47.146 --> 09:49.446 align:middle
and nonhousing services,
which is a big part of it.

09:49.896 --> 09:55.666 align:middle
So that part, I think
is, is moving along well.

09:55.866 --> 09:58.206 align:middle
But there's just so
much that we don't know.

09:58.336 --> 10:02.266 align:middle
I think-and we're in a
good position to wait

10:02.266 --> 10:03.226 align:middle
and see, is the thing.

10:03.226 --> 10:04.456 align:middle
We don't have to be in a hurry.

10:04.456 --> 10:06.366 align:middle
The economy is-has
been resilient

10:06.366 --> 10:07.466 align:middle
and is doing fairly well.

10:07.736 --> 10:09.046 align:middle
Our policy is well positioned.

10:09.046 --> 10:10.036 align:middle
The costs of waiting

10:10.546 --> 10:14.006 align:middle
to see further are
fairly low, we think.

10:14.646 --> 10:16.216 align:middle
So that's what we're doing.

10:16.386 --> 10:17.726 align:middle
And, you know, we'll, we'll see.

10:18.446 --> 10:22.456 align:middle
The Administration is
entering into negotiations

10:22.456 --> 10:24.096 align:middle
with many countries
over tariffs.

10:24.096 --> 10:28.186 align:middle
We'll know more with each
week and month that goes

10:28.186 --> 10:31.396 align:middle
by where-about where tariffs are
going to land-you know, land,

10:32.086 --> 10:33.666 align:middle
and we'll know what
the effects will be

10:33.666 --> 10:34.886 align:middle
when we start to
see those things.

10:34.886 --> 10:36.146 align:middle
So we think we'll be learning.

10:36.416 --> 10:37.906 align:middle
I can't tell you how
long it will take.

10:38.526 --> 10:41.236 align:middle
But, for now, it does
seem like it's, it's,

10:41.236 --> 10:44.816 align:middle
it's a fairly clear decision for
us to wait and see and watch.

10:45.046 --> 10:45.436 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

10:45.516 --> 10:47.316 align:middle
So when you say that you
don't need to be in a hurry,

10:47.316 --> 10:51.996 align:middle
does that mean that-could the
outlook change in such a way

10:51.996 --> 10:54.066 align:middle
that a change in your
stance could be warranted

10:54.066 --> 10:55.196 align:middle
as soon as your next meeting?

10:56.326 --> 10:57.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:57.026 --> 11:02.096 align:middle
You know, as I said,
we're-we are comfortable

11:02.096 --> 11:03.376 align:middle
with our policy stance.

11:03.546 --> 11:05.986 align:middle
We think the right-we're
in the right place to wait

11:05.986 --> 11:07.146 align:middle
and see how things evolve.

11:07.776 --> 11:09.436 align:middle
We don't feel like we
need to be in a hurry.

11:09.516 --> 11:11.376 align:middle
We feel like it's
appropriate to be patient.

11:12.026 --> 11:15.186 align:middle
And, you know, when
things develop-of course,

11:15.186 --> 11:18.396 align:middle
we have a record of-we can move
quickly when that's appropriate.

11:18.926 --> 11:21.706 align:middle
But we think right now, the
appropriate thing to do is

11:21.706 --> 11:23.106 align:middle
to wait and see how
things evolve.

11:23.106 --> 11:24.226 align:middle
There's so much uncertainty.

11:24.226 --> 11:26.726 align:middle
If you talk to businesses
or market participants

11:27.076 --> 11:31.226 align:middle
or forecasters, everyone
is just-is just waiting

11:31.226 --> 11:34.386 align:middle
to see how developments play
out, and then we'll be able

11:34.386 --> 11:37.326 align:middle
to make a better assessment
of what the appropriate path

11:37.556 --> 11:38.846 align:middle
for monetary policy is.

11:39.326 --> 11:40.756 align:middle
So we're not in that
place, and, you know,

11:40.756 --> 11:44.086 align:middle
as that develops-and I can't
really give you a time frame

11:44.086 --> 11:44.356 align:middle
on that.

11:45.266 --> 11:45.626 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:46.236 --> 11:48.886 align:middle
Jonnelle. JONNELLE MARTE.

11:49.836 --> 11:51.286 align:middle
Jonnelle Marte with Bloomberg.

11:51.756 --> 11:55.366 align:middle
So, many economists have
been pricing in higher odds

11:55.366 --> 12:00.366 align:middle
of a recession, and several are
noting that it is more difficult

12:00.776 --> 12:03.536 align:middle
for the Fed to cut
rates preemptively,

12:03.536 --> 12:06.496 align:middle
given the higher risks
for higher inflation.

12:06.866 --> 12:10.706 align:middle
So, given the outlook,
do you still see a path

12:11.066 --> 12:13.716 align:middle
for a soft landing, and
what does that look like?

12:13.756 --> 12:15.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:15.126 --> 12:19.826 align:middle
Well, let me say-I
mean, so let's, let's,

12:19.826 --> 12:22.076 align:middle
let's look back and
see where we are.

12:22.076 --> 12:25.616 align:middle
So, go through 2024 up
to the [present] day.

12:25.616 --> 12:28.496 align:middle
We've had-we've had, you know,
unemployment in the low 4s

12:28.496 --> 12:31.266 align:middle
for more than a year, we've had
inflation coming down in the-now

12:31.266 --> 12:34.616 align:middle
in the mid-to-low 2s, and
we've had an economy growing

12:34.616 --> 12:35.506 align:middle
at 2½ percent.

12:35.506 --> 12:39.366 align:middle
So that is-that is the
economy as we see it now.

12:40.466 --> 12:44.716 align:middle
What looks likely, given the
scope and scale of the tariffs,

12:45.116 --> 12:48.566 align:middle
is that we will see
the-certainly,

12:48.566 --> 12:49.986 align:middle
the risks to higher inflation,

12:49.986 --> 12:52.126 align:middle
higher unemployment
have increased.

12:52.126 --> 12:53.456 align:middle
And if that's what we do see,

12:53.556 --> 12:55.856 align:middle
if-and if the tariffs are
ultimately put in place

12:55.856 --> 12:58.456 align:middle
at those levels, which
we don't know, then,

12:58.556 --> 13:01.306 align:middle
then we will see-we won't see
further progress toward our

13:01.306 --> 13:03.936 align:middle
goals, but we might
see a delay in that.

13:03.936 --> 13:06.206 align:middle
I think in the-you
know, in our thinking,

13:06.206 --> 13:08.786 align:middle
we would get-we would
never-we never do anything

13:08.786 --> 13:10.166 align:middle
but keep achieving those goals.

13:10.166 --> 13:13.236 align:middle
But what we would-at least
for the next, let's say, year,

13:13.836 --> 13:16.206 align:middle
we would-we would not be making
progress toward those goals,

13:16.316 --> 13:18.886 align:middle
again, if that is-if that's
the way the tariffs shake out.

13:18.886 --> 13:20.126 align:middle
The thing is, we
don't know that.

13:20.126 --> 13:23.536 align:middle
There's so much uncertainty
about the scale, scope, timing,

13:23.536 --> 13:26.046 align:middle
and persistence of the tariffs.

13:26.546 --> 13:28.446 align:middle
So that's that.

13:28.446 --> 13:33.136 align:middle
In terms of preemption, you
know, I think you can look back

13:33.136 --> 13:36.346 align:middle
at the 2019 cuts as preemptive.

13:36.346 --> 13:39.106 align:middle
I wouldn't say that what we did
last fall was at all preemptive.

13:39.106 --> 13:41.096 align:middle
If anything, it was
a little late.

13:41.126 --> 13:44.406 align:middle
But 2019, we did
cut three times.

13:44.446 --> 13:46.976 align:middle
But the situation was, you
had a weakening economy,

13:46.976 --> 13:49.126 align:middle
and you had inflation
at 1.6 percent.

13:49.716 --> 13:52.546 align:middle
So that's a situation where
you can move preemptively.

13:52.546 --> 13:54.906 align:middle
Now we have inflation
running above target.

13:54.906 --> 13:56.816 align:middle
It has been above
target for four years.

13:57.296 --> 13:59.066 align:middle
It's not so far above
target now.

13:59.216 --> 14:02.896 align:middle
And we have an expectation
conditional on what happens

14:03.266 --> 14:05.766 align:middle
that we'll see upward
pressure on inflation.

14:05.766 --> 14:07.256 align:middle
If you look at where
forecasters are,

14:07.256 --> 14:09.876 align:middle
they're all forecasting
an increase in inflation.

14:09.876 --> 14:12.986 align:middle
So it makes it-and then
we've also got, you know,

14:12.986 --> 14:15.466 align:middle
forecasts of weakening
in the economy,

14:15.656 --> 14:17.666 align:middle
and some have recession
forecasts.

14:17.666 --> 14:20.456 align:middle
We don't make a-we don't
make-publish a forecast about,

14:20.456 --> 14:20.996 align:middle
about that.

14:21.536 --> 14:22.686 align:middle
We don't publish a forecast

14:22.786 --> 14:24.476 align:middle
that assesses how
likely a recession is.

14:24.516 --> 14:27.366 align:middle
But, in any case,
it's not a situation

14:27.366 --> 14:28.366 align:middle
where we can be preemptive,

14:28.366 --> 14:31.876 align:middle
because we actually don't know
what the right response to,

14:32.606 --> 14:34.606 align:middle
to the data will be
until we see more data.

14:36.566 --> 14:37.506 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:39.276 --> 14:41.046 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

14:41.306 --> 14:42.626 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the New York Times.

14:43.006 --> 14:45.496 align:middle
How much weakness does the
Committee need to see, though,

14:45.496 --> 14:47.606 align:middle
in the labor market and
the economy more broadly

14:47.636 --> 14:49.236 align:middle
to lower interest rates again?

14:49.716 --> 14:52.156 align:middle
Is it about a certain increase
in the unemployment rate

14:52.566 --> 14:56.466 align:middle
over a period of time, or
perhaps a certain number

14:56.466 --> 14:58.766 align:middle
of negative monthly job reports?

14:58.766 --> 15:00.446 align:middle
I mean, how are you
making that assessment?

15:00.666 --> 15:01.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:01.156 --> 15:03.196 align:middle
You know, so first of all,
we don't see that yet, right?

15:03.196 --> 15:04.936 align:middle
We have 4.2 percent
unemployment,

15:04.966 --> 15:07.876 align:middle
good participation,
wages behaving very well,

15:08.366 --> 15:10.676 align:middle
participation, I
mentioned, at a good level.

15:10.676 --> 15:13.316 align:middle
So, you know, with the
labor market, we would look

15:13.316 --> 15:15.626 align:middle
at the totality of
the-of the data.

15:15.626 --> 15:17.986 align:middle
We'd look at the level
of the unemployment rate.

15:17.986 --> 15:19.906 align:middle
We'd look at the speed
with which it's changing.

15:20.536 --> 15:25.166 align:middle
We would look at the whole huge
array of labor market data to,

15:25.316 --> 15:27.876 align:middle
to get a sense of whether
conditions are really

15:27.876 --> 15:29.176 align:middle
deteriorating or not.

15:29.266 --> 15:31.396 align:middle
And, at the same
time, we'd be looking

15:31.396 --> 15:32.516 align:middle
at the other side
of the mandate.

15:32.516 --> 15:33.936 align:middle
We could be in a
position of having

15:33.936 --> 15:36.036 align:middle
to balance those two
things, which is, of course,

15:36.036 --> 15:40.146 align:middle
a very-a difficult balancing
judgment that we'd have to make.

15:40.716 --> 15:41.036 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

15:41.036 --> 15:43.516 align:middle
On that point about balancing,
I mean, you've mentioned

15:43.546 --> 15:47.136 align:middle
that the Committee would
consider how far the economy is

15:47.136 --> 15:49.126 align:middle
from each goal and the
time it would take to kind

15:49.126 --> 15:50.336 align:middle
of get back to that point.

15:50.426 --> 15:52.286 align:middle
But what does that
mean in practice?

15:52.286 --> 15:54.056 align:middle
I mean, how much of that
assessment will be rooted

15:54.276 --> 15:56.526 align:middle
in a forecast, versus
data dependence?

15:57.276 --> 15:58.056 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:58.146 --> 15:59.736 align:middle
It would be a combination
of the two.

15:59.736 --> 16:03.806 align:middle
I mean, let's just say this is
a-this would be a complicated

16:03.806 --> 16:05.846 align:middle
and challenging judgment
that we would have

16:05.846 --> 16:09.096 align:middle
to make if-and this is-we're
not in this situation,

16:09.096 --> 16:11.316 align:middle
but the situation is if the
two goals are in tension.

16:11.316 --> 16:14.386 align:middle
So let's say that
unemployment is moving

16:14.386 --> 16:15.716 align:middle
up in an uncomfortable way

16:15.716 --> 16:17.796 align:middle
and so is inflation-not
the situation

16:17.796 --> 16:19.216 align:middle
we're in-hypothetically.

16:19.216 --> 16:22.066 align:middle
But we would look at how
far they are from the goals,

16:22.066 --> 16:23.696 align:middle
how far they're expected
to be from the goals,

16:23.696 --> 16:26.566 align:middle
what's the expected time
to get back to their goals.

16:27.056 --> 16:29.156 align:middle
We'd look at all those things
and make a difficult judgment.

16:29.156 --> 16:30.656 align:middle
And that's in our framework.

16:30.836 --> 16:32.166 align:middle
It's always been
in our thinking.

16:32.556 --> 16:34.476 align:middle
You know, it's-we haven't
had-we haven't faced

16:34.526 --> 16:37.376 align:middle
that question in
a very long time.

16:37.486 --> 16:41.436 align:middle
And so, again, difficult,
difficult judgment to make,

16:41.436 --> 16:42.796 align:middle
and not one that we face today.

16:42.796 --> 16:44.426 align:middle
And we may never face it.

16:44.486 --> 16:47.796 align:middle
But, you know, we have to be
keeping it in our thinking now.

16:48.316 --> 16:49.366 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

16:49.366 --> 16:52.026 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

16:52.066 --> 16:52.506 align:middle
Thank you.

16:52.816 --> 16:54.966 align:middle
Edward Lawrence with
Fox Business.

16:55.346 --> 16:58.306 align:middle
So we had the CPI report
that came out that showed,

16:58.306 --> 16:59.856 align:middle
month over month,
the first increase

16:59.856 --> 17:01.836 align:middle
in inflation in about
three years.

17:01.836 --> 17:04.206 align:middle
The jobs report-you said
"solid"-that we saw.

17:04.206 --> 17:05.686 align:middle
At the same time, we
have those new tariffs

17:05.686 --> 17:06.476 align:middle
that we're living under.

17:06.826 --> 17:10.066 align:middle
So, given this, should the
Federal Reserve be cutting rates

17:10.066 --> 17:10.656 align:middle
at all this year?

17:13.676 --> 17:14.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:14.076 --> 17:14.996 align:middle
You know, it's going to depend.

17:14.996 --> 17:16.936 align:middle
I think you have to
just take a step back

17:16.936 --> 17:20.876 align:middle
and realize this is-this is
why we are where we are, is,

17:20.956 --> 17:23.416 align:middle
you know, we are going to
need to see how this evolves.

17:23.796 --> 17:26.316 align:middle
There are cases in which it
would be appropriate for us

17:26.316 --> 17:27.426 align:middle
to cut rates this year.

17:27.546 --> 17:28.866 align:middle
There are cases in
which it wouldn't,

17:29.506 --> 17:30.626 align:middle
and we just don't know.

17:30.626 --> 17:34.306 align:middle
Until we know more about how
this is going to settle out

17:34.946 --> 17:37.956 align:middle
and what the economic
implications are for employment,

17:38.036 --> 17:44.606 align:middle
for-and for, for inflation,
I couldn't confidently say

17:45.156 --> 17:47.766 align:middle
that I know what the
appropriate path will be.

17:48.676 --> 17:49.296 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

17:49.296 --> 17:50.176 align:middle
So, following on that, just,

17:50.176 --> 17:53.046 align:middle
you know-so then how does
President Trump calling

17:53.046 --> 17:54.796 align:middle
on you personally as well
as the Federal Reserve

17:55.156 --> 17:57.436 align:middle
to make rate cuts affect
your decision today

17:57.436 --> 17:59.116 align:middle
and affect your job difficulty?

17:59.116 --> 18:00.256 align:middle
[0:18:00] CHAIR POWELL.

18:00.256 --> 18:01.996 align:middle
Doesn't affect our-doing
our job at all.

18:02.286 --> 18:03.586 align:middle
So we-you know, we're
always going

18:03.586 --> 18:07.426 align:middle
to do the same thing-which is,
we're going to use our tools

18:07.426 --> 18:10.776 align:middle
to foster maximum employment and
price stability for the benefit

18:10.776 --> 18:11.606 align:middle
of the American people.

18:11.746 --> 18:14.936 align:middle
We're always going to consider
only the economic data,

18:15.026 --> 18:16.736 align:middle
the outlook, the
balance of risks.

18:17.286 --> 18:18.816 align:middle
And that's it, that's all
we're going to consider.

18:18.816 --> 18:20.976 align:middle
So it really doesn't
affect either our job

18:20.976 --> 18:21.786 align:middle
or the way we do it.

18:23.186 --> 18:26.196 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

18:26.346 --> 18:27.446 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

18:27.446 --> 18:28.676 align:middle
Hi. Howard Schneider
with Reuters,

18:28.676 --> 18:29.866 align:middle
and thanks for the time.

18:30.236 --> 18:32.096 align:middle
I'm wondering-I mean,
given the complexity

18:32.096 --> 18:35.206 align:middle
about the first-quarter
GDP and what lies ahead,

18:35.206 --> 18:37.356 align:middle
I'm just wondering what
your intuition tells you

18:37.356 --> 18:40.806 align:middle
about the underlying direction
of the economy right now.

18:40.806 --> 18:43.336 align:middle
Many of your colleagues
have said they feel growth

18:43.336 --> 18:44.516 align:middle
is slowing.

18:44.516 --> 18:47.706 align:middle
If so, do you have any
sense of by how much,

18:47.706 --> 18:49.236 align:middle
to what degree the
slowdown may be?

18:49.796 --> 18:50.756 align:middle
What does your gut tell you

18:50.756 --> 18:52.356 align:middle
about how things are
evolving out there?

18:52.686 --> 18:53.356 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:53.546 --> 18:56.096 align:middle
My gut tells me that
uncertainty about the path

18:56.096 --> 18:58.416 align:middle
of the economy is
extremely elevated [laughter]

18:59.546 --> 19:03.656 align:middle
and that the downside
risks have increased.

19:04.356 --> 19:07.196 align:middle
The risk is-as we pointed
out in our statement,

19:07.196 --> 19:10.476 align:middle
the risks-the risks
of higher unemployment

19:10.476 --> 19:12.026 align:middle
and higher inflation have risen,

19:12.886 --> 19:14.736 align:middle
but they haven't
materialized yet.

19:14.916 --> 19:15.546 align:middle
They really haven't.

19:15.546 --> 19:17.616 align:middle
They're not, really
not in the data yet,

19:17.616 --> 19:21.116 align:middle
so that-and that tells
me more than by intuition

19:21.116 --> 19:23.406 align:middle
because I think-I think
it's obvious, actually,

19:23.406 --> 19:27.136 align:middle
that the right thing for us to
do is to-we're in a good place.

19:27.136 --> 19:30.306 align:middle
Our policy's in a very good
place, and the right thing

19:30.356 --> 19:33.576 align:middle
to do is, is await
further clarity.

19:34.186 --> 19:38.346 align:middle
And I-you know, there's-usually,
things clarify,

19:38.486 --> 19:41.066 align:middle
and that-the appropriate
direction becomes clear.

19:41.066 --> 19:42.556 align:middle
That's what usually
happens, right?

19:42.556 --> 19:44.776 align:middle
Right now, it's very hard
to say what that would be.

19:44.776 --> 19:47.356 align:middle
In the meantime, the
economy is doing fine.

19:47.986 --> 19:50.926 align:middle
Our policy isn't-you know,
it's not highly restrictive.

19:50.926 --> 19:52.036 align:middle
It's somewhat restrictive.

19:52.036 --> 19:54.526 align:middle
It's 100 basis points less
restrictive than it was in,

19:54.876 --> 19:55.876 align:middle
you know, last summer.

19:56.426 --> 19:58.106 align:middle
So we think it's-we think
it's in a good place,

19:58.106 --> 20:00.856 align:middle
and we think the appropriate
thing is for us to wait and see

20:01.006 --> 20:03.066 align:middle
and get more clarity about,

20:03.176 --> 20:04.616 align:middle
about the direction
of the economy.

20:04.616 --> 20:05.046 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

20:05.046 --> 20:06.516 align:middle
Well, let me press
you on this idea

20:06.516 --> 20:08.666 align:middle
of the economy being
fine right now, because,

20:08.806 --> 20:12.436 align:middle
reading the Beige Book very
closely the last time around,

20:12.556 --> 20:15.586 align:middle
there was a lot of, you
know, negative stuff,

20:15.586 --> 20:17.176 align:middle
negative sentiment
that was in there.

20:17.176 --> 20:20.266 align:middle
And I know that everybody's
looking at soft data right now.

20:20.816 --> 20:23.486 align:middle
You mentioned it yourself-that
the sentiment's sour.

20:23.976 --> 20:26.866 align:middle
But the Beige Book was talking
about, you know, the beginnings

20:26.866 --> 20:30.446 align:middle
of layoffs in some
industries, prices rising in,

20:30.446 --> 20:34.026 align:middle
in some places, and an awful lot
of investment decisions being,

20:34.106 --> 20:35.436 align:middle
being pushed to the sideline.

20:35.466 --> 20:37.766 align:middle
Doesn't that point
to a slowdown?

20:37.966 --> 20:38.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:39.126 --> 20:39.916 align:middle
It may well.

20:40.076 --> 20:41.566 align:middle
It just hasn't shown up yet.

20:41.776 --> 20:46.136 align:middle
And, and, you know, we all
look at all these sentiments

20:46.136 --> 20:48.236 align:middle
and read many, many
individual comments just

20:48.236 --> 20:49.716 align:middle
to get a better feel.

20:49.716 --> 20:51.576 align:middle
And it-you know, businesses

20:51.576 --> 20:57.026 align:middle
and households very broadly
are concerned and, you know,

20:57.026 --> 20:59.186 align:middle
postponing economic
decisions of various kinds.

20:59.186 --> 21:03.176 align:middle
And, yes, if that continues and
nothing, nothing happens to sort

21:03.176 --> 21:05.806 align:middle
of alleviate those concerns,
then you would expect

21:05.806 --> 21:08.386 align:middle
that to begin to show
up in economic data.

21:08.386 --> 21:10.896 align:middle
It wouldn't maybe show up
overnight, but it would show

21:10.896 --> 21:12.076 align:middle
up over weeks and months.

21:12.746 --> 21:14.976 align:middle
And that may be what happens,
but it hasn't happened yet.

21:15.306 --> 21:16.876 align:middle
And also, there are
things that can happen

21:16.876 --> 21:19.356 align:middle
that will-that will
change that narrative.

21:19.356 --> 21:20.376 align:middle
I mean, they haven't happened,

21:20.476 --> 21:22.506 align:middle
but it's possible
to imagine things.

21:22.506 --> 21:25.956 align:middle
But in the meantime, yes, we're
watching it extremely carefully,

21:25.956 --> 21:29.626 align:middle
like everyone is, but don't
see really much evidence of it

21:29.626 --> 21:32.106 align:middle
in the actual economic data yet.

21:32.796 --> 21:35.926 align:middle
And by the way, consumers keep
spending, credit card spending.

21:35.926 --> 21:39.826 align:middle
It's-you know, it's still-it's
still a healthy economy,

21:40.186 --> 21:44.226 align:middle
albeit one that is shrouded in
some very downbeat sentiment

21:44.226 --> 21:47.246 align:middle
on the part of people
and businesses.

21:47.846 --> 21:49.256 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

21:49.256 --> 21:49.486 align:middle
Thank you.

21:49.516 --> 21:51.126 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:51.256 --> 21:51.756 align:middle
Michael McKee.

21:51.846 --> 21:52.156 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

21:52.746 --> 21:54.546 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

21:54.986 --> 21:57.646 align:middle
The Fed's been criticized
recently by a former Governor

21:57.646 --> 22:00.236 align:middle
for what he calls
"mission creep"-you take

22:00.236 --> 22:02.946 align:middle
on more problems, use
more tools, and then end

22:02.946 --> 22:06.006 align:middle
up building tools to deal with
the fallout of those tools,

22:06.486 --> 22:07.846 align:middle
which then makes it a given

22:07.846 --> 22:10.126 align:middle
that you will act more
aggressively in the future.

22:10.516 --> 22:11.736 align:middle
Is that a fair critique,

22:11.736 --> 22:13.346 align:middle
and is that something
you would be looking

22:13.346 --> 22:15.306 align:middle
at in your framework review?

22:15.306 --> 22:15.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:15.556 --> 22:16.676 align:middle
Sorry, say that critique again.

22:17.306 --> 22:17.876 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

22:17.876 --> 22:20.536 align:middle
That the Fed has been involved
in mission creep-gets involved

22:20.856 --> 22:23.556 align:middle
in using too many
new tools to deal

22:23.556 --> 22:26.406 align:middle
with problems and to go too far.

22:26.406 --> 22:32.306 align:middle
Is that something
that-the critique was based

22:32.306 --> 22:37.676 align:middle
around the fact that you did QE
and QE and QE and- CHAIR POWELL.

22:37.756 --> 22:38.576 align:middle
Oh, okay. Sure.

22:38.576 --> 22:38.936 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

22:39.146 --> 22:43.106 align:middle
-and went beyond the narrow
confines of your mandate.

22:43.106 --> 22:43.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:43.616 --> 22:46.286 align:middle
So-well, I mean, there are a
couple-that's not really beyond

22:46.286 --> 22:47.226 align:middle
the confines of our mandate.

22:47.226 --> 22:50.816 align:middle
I-look, I would say this: We-you
know, we did things-essentially,

22:50.816 --> 22:53.386 align:middle
we were on an emergency
footing for a couple of years

22:53.386 --> 22:56.916 align:middle
in the pandemic, and it's very
fair and very welcome for people

22:56.916 --> 22:59.306 align:middle
to look back over what
we did and say, "Hey,

22:59.306 --> 23:00.736 align:middle
you could have done this
better and different."

23:01.206 --> 23:02.366 align:middle
And one thing we hear a lot is,

23:02.366 --> 23:04.666 align:middle
we could have explained
the QE a little better.

23:04.926 --> 23:07.816 align:middle
We did think we were
explaining it in real time.

23:08.196 --> 23:09.396 align:middle
I completely accept the,

23:09.496 --> 23:11.716 align:middle
the thought that we could
have explained it better.

23:12.236 --> 23:15.506 align:middle
There's a lot of thinking that
we went on too long with QE.

23:15.506 --> 23:18.236 align:middle
I can tell you that
the reason we did was,

23:18.236 --> 23:19.746 align:middle
we were-we were concerned

23:19.966 --> 23:23.546 align:middle
that we didn't want a
tightening of-a sharp tightening

23:23.546 --> 23:25.186 align:middle
in financial conditions
at a time

23:25.186 --> 23:27.046 align:middle
when we thought the economy
was still vulnerable.

23:27.316 --> 23:30.916 align:middle
And so we did hold on
for a long time to QE.

23:30.916 --> 23:32.366 align:middle
And we, of course,
tapered and everything.

23:32.366 --> 23:35.886 align:middle
And then we immediately
went into QT, and, you know,

23:35.886 --> 23:37.666 align:middle
we've-we're down a
couple of trillion.

23:37.666 --> 23:40.206 align:middle
But I get the-that, you
know, we, we certainly,

23:40.206 --> 23:41.296 align:middle
with the benefit of hindsight,

23:41.356 --> 23:43.436 align:middle
could have tapered
earlier or faster.

23:44.106 --> 23:45.626 align:middle
That's absolutely right.

23:45.626 --> 23:46.956 align:middle
But this is all very welcome.

23:46.996 --> 23:49.216 align:middle
You know, it's, it's-you
know, we knew,

23:49.266 --> 23:51.816 align:middle
doing this in real time, that we
weren't going to get it perfect.

23:51.816 --> 23:56.956 align:middle
And those kind of-those kind
of, you know, after-action kind

23:57.736 --> 23:59.566 align:middle
of looks are essential.

23:59.566 --> 24:00.956 align:middle
And we're doing the
same thing in-you know,

24:00.956 --> 24:03.516 align:middle
in our review, so-on
some issues.

24:03.726 --> 24:04.406 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

24:04.406 --> 24:07.276 align:middle
There-the other part of that
critique is that you've taken

24:07.276 --> 24:10.086 align:middle
on topics that are
outside of the mandate,

24:10.186 --> 24:15.186 align:middle
such as climate change
and trying to ensure

24:15.186 --> 24:18.286 align:middle
that certain groups
are benefited

24:18.366 --> 24:22.456 align:middle
by your economic policies in
terms of employment, et cetera.

24:23.006 --> 24:23.686 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:23.686 --> 24:27.696 align:middle
So, okay, on climate, you've
heard me say over and over again

24:27.696 --> 24:32.816 align:middle
that we will not be climate
policymakers and that our role

24:33.066 --> 24:35.566 align:middle
on climate is a very,
very narrow one.

24:35.566 --> 24:37.876 align:middle
And I think-I think
that's what we've done.

24:37.876 --> 24:40.176 align:middle
We've done really very
little on climate.

24:40.176 --> 24:42.956 align:middle
You can say that
was-that little bit

24:42.956 --> 24:44.566 align:middle
that we've done was too much.

24:44.566 --> 24:47.686 align:middle
But I wouldn't want to give any
impression that, that, you know,

24:47.686 --> 24:50.356 align:middle
we take-that we've taken
climate in and it's something

24:50.356 --> 24:53.066 align:middle
that we're spending a lot
of time and energy on.

24:53.066 --> 24:53.476 align:middle
We're not.

24:53.886 --> 24:55.596 align:middle
We have very, very
narrow things.

24:55.596 --> 24:59.536 align:middle
We did one thing, one
guidance for the banks,

24:59.536 --> 25:03.906 align:middle
and then we did one-a
one-time stress analysis,

25:03.906 --> 25:05.396 align:middle
climate stress analysis,
and that's it.

25:05.506 --> 25:07.396 align:middle
And, you know, we
dropped out of the Network

25:07.396 --> 25:08.656 align:middle
for Greening the
Financial System.

25:09.466 --> 25:13.746 align:middle
So we didn't do much on
climate, but-and I do think,

25:13.746 --> 25:17.636 align:middle
and I've said this publicly
several times, I think it's,

25:17.716 --> 25:21.056 align:middle
it's a real danger for us to try
to take on a mandate like that,

25:21.056 --> 25:25.076 align:middle
which is very narrow
application to our work.

25:25.076 --> 25:28.516 align:middle
And, you know, the risk is,
if you-if you go for things

25:28.516 --> 25:30.336 align:middle
that are really not on
your mandate, you know,

25:30.726 --> 25:31.846 align:middle
then why are you independent?

25:32.336 --> 25:34.506 align:middle
You know, I think that's
a very fair question.

25:34.506 --> 25:36.976 align:middle
And I do think we've done
a whole lot less on climate

25:37.006 --> 25:38.566 align:middle
than some people
seem to think we did.

25:39.296 --> 25:41.006 align:middle
Anyway, that's what I think.

25:41.006 --> 25:41.296 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

25:41.296 --> 25:44.806 align:middle
Should you have taken
on the question

25:44.916 --> 25:47.606 align:middle
of bringing unemployment rates
down for specific categories?

25:47.606 --> 25:47.846 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:47.846 --> 25:48.696 align:middle
We didn't do that.

25:48.696 --> 25:50.136 align:middle
You know, we said-what
we said was

25:50.136 --> 25:53.936 align:middle
that we never targeted
any unemployment rate

25:53.936 --> 25:56.106 align:middle
for individual racial
or demographic groups.

25:56.106 --> 25:59.506 align:middle
What we said was that
maximum employment was a broad

25:59.506 --> 26:00.506 align:middle
and inclusive goal.

26:00.686 --> 26:03.126 align:middle
And I think what we
meant by that was,

26:03.126 --> 26:07.386 align:middle
we're going to consider
the totality of the country

26:07.946 --> 26:11.136 align:middle
as we look at our
maximum-employment goal.

26:11.136 --> 26:12.686 align:middle
Of course, we were
never going to target

26:12.686 --> 26:15.266 align:middle
on any individual group with
that, but I think some people,

26:15.996 --> 26:17.656 align:middle
you know, wanted to
hear it that way.

26:17.656 --> 26:19.296 align:middle
But that's not at
all what we meant.

26:19.986 --> 26:23.846 align:middle
But-so that's just
not a correct reading

26:23.976 --> 26:26.886 align:middle
of our-but I can see how,
you know, maybe people found

26:26.886 --> 26:28.766 align:middle
that confusing, and, you
know, we have to take

26:28.766 --> 26:29.666 align:middle
that into consideration.

26:30.746 --> 26:31.386 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

26:31.706 --> 26:34.786 align:middle
Jo Ling. JO LING KENT.

26:34.786 --> 26:35.346 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

26:35.346 --> 26:36.826 align:middle
Thanks for taking
our questions today.

26:36.896 --> 26:39.206 align:middle
I'm Jo Ling Kent with CBS News.

26:39.206 --> 26:40.506 align:middle
You've said, "Wait and see"

26:40.846 --> 26:42.526 align:middle
and "The economy is
doing fine" today.

26:42.526 --> 26:45.536 align:middle
But the impact of tariffs are
already showing up at the ports.

26:45.996 --> 26:48.606 align:middle
Businesses big and small are
telling us that they feel it,

26:48.816 --> 26:50.886 align:middle
and most importantly,
consumers say they feel it.

26:51.196 --> 26:52.656 align:middle
But the challenges are here.

26:52.656 --> 26:54.366 align:middle
And there's no waiting
and seeing.

26:54.906 --> 26:57.466 align:middle
For Main Street, what
is the breaking point?

26:57.466 --> 27:01.156 align:middle
What would have to happen to
prompt a rate cut specifically?

27:01.686 --> 27:02.096 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:02.096 --> 27:07.626 align:middle
Well, you know, so we
really don't see in the data

27:07.936 --> 27:09.406 align:middle
yet big economic effects.

27:09.406 --> 27:12.606 align:middle
We see-we see sentiment,
their concerns

27:13.016 --> 27:15.746 align:middle
that higher prices may be
coming, or things like that,

27:15.856 --> 27:18.446 align:middle
but-so people, they're
worried now about inflation.

27:18.446 --> 27:22.286 align:middle
They're worried about, you
know, a shock from the tariffs.

27:22.286 --> 27:24.506 align:middle
But they really haven't-that
shock hasn't hit yet.

27:25.006 --> 27:27.926 align:middle
Okay, so, you know,
we're going to be looking

27:27.926 --> 27:31.286 align:middle
at not just the sentiment data,
but also the real economic data

27:31.836 --> 27:34.376 align:middle
as we assess what
it is we should do.

27:34.376 --> 27:36.426 align:middle
And remember, we're-there
will be two effects.

27:36.676 --> 27:40.076 align:middle
One of them would be weakening
economy-weakening economic

27:40.076 --> 27:42.906 align:middle
activity, which translates
into higher unemployment,

27:43.186 --> 27:45.726 align:middle
and the other would be
potentially higher inflation.

27:45.946 --> 27:50.466 align:middle
Again, to say it again, the
timing, the scope, the scale,

27:50.466 --> 27:52.586 align:middle
and the persistence of
those effects are very,

27:52.586 --> 27:53.466 align:middle
very uncertain.

27:53.566 --> 27:57.266 align:middle
So it's not at all clear
what the appropriate response

27:57.266 --> 27:59.986 align:middle
for monetary policy
is at this time.

28:00.236 --> 28:02.686 align:middle
And we're-you know-and
by the way, our policy's

28:02.686 --> 28:05.336 align:middle
in a good place, so we
think we can wait and move

28:05.336 --> 28:07.506 align:middle
when it is clear what
the right thing to do is;

28:07.796 --> 28:10.206 align:middle
really not at all clear
what it is we should do.

28:10.736 --> 28:14.136 align:middle
So people are feeling
stress and concern,

28:14.256 --> 28:15.686 align:middle
but unemployment hasn't gone up.

28:15.906 --> 28:17.186 align:middle
Job creation is fine.

28:17.526 --> 28:18.776 align:middle
Wages are in good shape.

28:18.776 --> 28:22.956 align:middle
You know, people are not-layoffs
are-people are not getting laid

28:22.956 --> 28:24.036 align:middle
off at high levels.

28:25.316 --> 28:28.296 align:middle
You know, initial claims for
unemployment are not increasing,

28:28.296 --> 28:31.116 align:middle
you know, in any kind
of impressive way.

28:31.116 --> 28:35.316 align:middle
So the economy itself is still,
you know, in solid shape.

28:35.476 --> 28:36.246 align:middle
JO LING KENT.

28:36.286 --> 28:37.196 align:middle
Just a quick follow-up:

28:37.676 --> 28:42.096 align:middle
President Trump now says he does
not plan to remove you as Chair.

28:42.546 --> 28:44.466 align:middle
When you heard that,
what did you think?

28:44.466 --> 28:45.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:45.146 --> 28:46.906 align:middle
I don't have anything
more for you on that.

28:46.906 --> 28:48.806 align:middle
I've pretty much
covered that issue.

28:48.876 --> 28:49.166 align:middle
Thank you.

28:49.816 --> 28:50.656 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

28:52.696 --> 28:53.706 align:middle
Chris. CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

28:53.706 --> 28:54.336 align:middle
Hi. Thank you.

28:54.546 --> 28:56.016 align:middle
Chris Rugaber, Associated Press.

28:56.016 --> 28:58.166 align:middle
Well, I just wanted
to follow up.

28:58.166 --> 29:02.846 align:middle
Earlier, it sounded like you
said it was unclear how the

29:02.846 --> 29:04.206 align:middle
Fed-you know, what kind

29:04.206 --> 29:06.376 align:middle
of interest rate decisions
you will make later this year.

29:06.376 --> 29:09.896 align:middle
So does that-you know, in
March, there was guidance

29:09.896 --> 29:11.796 align:middle
that two cuts might
happen, you know,

29:11.796 --> 29:13.336 align:middle
two cuts were penciled
in for this year.

29:13.336 --> 29:16.516 align:middle
Is that now-that guidance from
the last press conference,

29:16.516 --> 29:18.876 align:middle
has that been overtaken
by events at this point?

29:20.316 --> 29:20.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:20.996 --> 29:23.646 align:middle
You know, we don't do a
Summary of Economic Projections

29:23.646 --> 29:24.716 align:middle
at every meeting, as you know.

29:24.946 --> 29:26.186 align:middle
We do it every other meeting.

29:26.186 --> 29:28.026 align:middle
And so this was the meeting
when we didn't do it.

29:28.696 --> 29:33.236 align:middle
And I-you know, we don't
also kind of poll people,

29:33.236 --> 29:34.926 align:middle
so I really-I really
wouldn't want to try

29:34.926 --> 29:37.416 align:middle
to make a specific projection

29:37.416 --> 29:39.536 align:middle
for where we are
relative to that.

29:39.536 --> 29:41.656 align:middle
We will-in six weeks, we
have the June meeting,

29:41.656 --> 29:43.166 align:middle
and you'll have another SEP.

29:43.166 --> 29:47.786 align:middle
I'm not going to hazard a
guess here today what-as

29:47.786 --> 29:48.476 align:middle
to what it would be.

29:48.966 --> 29:50.626 align:middle
Again, what I would say is

29:50.816 --> 29:54.006 align:middle
that we think our policy
rate is in a good place.

29:54.006 --> 29:55.966 align:middle
We think it leaves us
well positioned to respond

29:55.966 --> 29:58.676 align:middle
in a timely way to
potential developments.

29:58.746 --> 30:01.526 align:middle
That's where we are,
and that-depending

30:01.526 --> 30:02.796 align:middle
on the way things play out,

30:02.866 --> 30:06.266 align:middle
that could include rate
hikes-sorry, rate cuts.

30:06.346 --> 30:10.296 align:middle
You know, it could include
us holding where we are.

30:10.776 --> 30:14.936 align:middle
We just are going to need to
see, you know, how things play

30:14.936 --> 30:16.626 align:middle
out before we make
those decisions.

30:16.626 --> 30:17.416 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

30:17.946 --> 30:20.166 align:middle
Great. And just to
follow up on that, I mean,

30:20.526 --> 30:22.136 align:middle
when you address the issue

30:22.136 --> 30:25.016 align:middle
of how the Fed would handle
both rising unemployment

30:25.016 --> 30:27.716 align:middle
and rising inflation, how are
you thinking about the fact

30:27.746 --> 30:30.536 align:middle
that addressing one could
exacerbate the other?

30:30.536 --> 30:33.676 align:middle
So a rate cut to reduce
unemployment could worsen

30:33.676 --> 30:34.726 align:middle
inflation, and vice versa.

30:34.726 --> 30:37.596 align:middle
How does that-how do you
handle those challenges?

30:37.596 --> 30:37.916 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:37.916 --> 30:40.016 align:middle
Well, you just captured
the-this is the issue

30:40.016 --> 30:42.736 align:middle
with the two goals
being in tension.

30:42.736 --> 30:44.816 align:middle
It's a very challenging
question.

30:44.816 --> 30:46.006 align:middle
Now, there can be a case

30:46.006 --> 30:50.726 align:middle
in which one goal is very
far-one variable's very far

30:50.776 --> 30:52.636 align:middle
from its goal, much
farther than the other.

30:52.746 --> 30:54.446 align:middle
And if so, you concentrate
on that one.

30:54.856 --> 30:58.606 align:middle
And, frankly, that was the
case-well, it wasn't a case

30:58.606 --> 30:59.696 align:middle
where they were really
in tension.

30:59.696 --> 31:03.516 align:middle
But if you go back to 2022, it
was very clear that we needed

31:03.516 --> 31:04.616 align:middle
to focus on inflation.

31:04.806 --> 31:07.366 align:middle
The labor market was
also super tight,

31:07.446 --> 31:10.136 align:middle
so it wasn't really a tradeoff.

31:10.586 --> 31:14.666 align:middle
You know, if-I think you know
what our-what our framework

31:14.666 --> 31:15.496 align:middle
document says.

31:15.496 --> 31:18.646 align:middle
It says we'll look at how far
each goal-each variable is

31:18.646 --> 31:20.606 align:middle
from its goal and
also we'll factor

31:20.606 --> 31:22.156 align:middle
in the time it would
take to get there.

31:22.706 --> 31:25.976 align:middle
So, you know, that's
going to be a-potentially,

31:25.976 --> 31:27.816 align:middle
a very difficult judgment.

31:28.516 --> 31:30.446 align:middle
But the data could break
in a way that it's not.

31:30.726 --> 31:32.046 align:middle
You know, I just don't
think we know that.

31:32.286 --> 31:34.576 align:middle
The data could easily
favor one or the other.

31:35.196 --> 31:39.496 align:middle
And right now, there's no way
to-no need to make a choice

31:39.496 --> 31:42.346 align:middle
and no real basis for doing so.

31:44.016 --> 31:45.986 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:46.336 --> 31:47.426 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

31:47.586 --> 31:49.096 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

31:49.196 --> 31:53.156 align:middle
I wanted to ask, Congress is
currently debating spending cuts

31:53.156 --> 31:55.666 align:middle
alongside expending-extending
the tax cuts.

31:56.106 --> 31:58.366 align:middle
And I know you've talked
many times about how the path

31:58.366 --> 32:00.186 align:middle
of the debt is unsustainable.

32:00.186 --> 32:02.186 align:middle
But given that we're
also talking right now

32:02.186 --> 32:04.966 align:middle
about the economy slowing,
potentially even recession,

32:05.306 --> 32:06.806 align:middle
I was just wondering,
is there a danger

32:06.806 --> 32:09.606 align:middle
that spending cuts now could
slow growth a lot more?

32:10.046 --> 32:10.316 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:10.356 --> 32:14.216 align:middle
You know, we don't give
Congress fiscal advice.

32:15.366 --> 32:18.326 align:middle
I-they're going to do-we
take what they do as a given,

32:18.326 --> 32:20.986 align:middle
and we put it in our
models and in our assessment

32:20.986 --> 32:23.026 align:middle
of the economy, so
I wouldn't want to,

32:23.516 --> 32:24.586 align:middle
you know, speculate on that.

32:24.836 --> 32:26.416 align:middle
I mean, I think we do
know that the debt is

32:26.416 --> 32:30.246 align:middle
on an unsustainable level-on
an unsustainable path-not

32:30.246 --> 32:32.286 align:middle
on an unsustainable level,
but an unsustainable path.

32:33.086 --> 32:37.336 align:middle
And it's on Congress to
figure out how to get us back

32:37.336 --> 32:38.826 align:middle
on a sustainable
path, and, you know,

32:38.826 --> 32:40.436 align:middle
it's not up to us
to give them advice.

32:40.436 --> 32:41.116 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

32:41.206 --> 32:42.956 align:middle
Well, do you think that they
should take macroeconomic

32:43.046 --> 32:45.806 align:middle
conditions into account
as they look at this?

32:46.166 --> 32:46.926 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:46.926 --> 32:49.866 align:middle
I think they don't need my
advice and our advice on how

32:49.996 --> 32:51.616 align:middle
to do fiscal policy any more

32:51.616 --> 32:53.426 align:middle
than we need their
advice on monetary policy.

32:53.696 --> 32:56.946 align:middle
[Laughter] MICHELLE SMITH.

32:57.396 --> 33:00.006 align:middle
Andrew. ANDREW ACKERMAN.

33:00.196 --> 33:01.016 align:middle
Thanks, Mr. Chairman.

33:01.096 --> 33:02.696 align:middle
Andrew Ackerman with
the Washington Post.

33:03.026 --> 33:05.286 align:middle
In your Jackson Hole
comments last year,

33:05.786 --> 33:07.566 align:middle
you said you would not
welcome further cooling

33:07.566 --> 33:08.986 align:middle
in labor market conditions.

33:09.046 --> 33:11.356 align:middle
The unemployment rate
then was 4.2 percent,

33:11.356 --> 33:12.376 align:middle
which is what it is now.

33:12.736 --> 33:16.786 align:middle
Forecasters-many forecasters now
predict a higher jobless rate.

33:17.086 --> 33:18.096 align:middle
How has your tolerance

33:18.096 --> 33:21.836 align:middle
for weakening labor market
conditions changed compared

33:21.836 --> 33:22.416 align:middle
to a year ago?

33:22.466 --> 33:22.876 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:22.876 --> 33:24.666 align:middle
So it was quite a
different situation.

33:24.726 --> 33:30.476 align:middle
What was happening last year
is that over the space of six,

33:30.476 --> 33:34.156 align:middle
eight, seven months, the
unemployment rate went

33:34.156 --> 33:37.286 align:middle
up by almost a full percentage
point, and it was click, click,

33:37.286 --> 33:38.496 align:middle
click, click, click each month.

33:38.696 --> 33:42.166 align:middle
And, you know, everywhere,
people were talking

33:42.166 --> 33:43.716 align:middle
about downside risks
to the labor market.

33:43.716 --> 33:48.106 align:middle
At the same time, payroll job
numbers were getting softer

33:48.106 --> 33:48.486 align:middle
and softer.

33:48.486 --> 33:50.936 align:middle
So there was a really
obvious concern

33:51.156 --> 33:53.806 align:middle
about downside risk
to the labor market.

33:53.906 --> 33:57.406 align:middle
And so at Jackson Hole and
then in September, you know,

33:57.406 --> 33:59.096 align:middle
we wanted to address
that forthrightly.

33:59.096 --> 34:00.986 align:middle
We wanted to show that we
were there for the-I mean,

34:00.986 --> 34:03.956 align:middle
we had been there for
inflation for a couple of years,

34:03.956 --> 34:05.036 align:middle
and we wanted to show also

34:05.036 --> 34:07.126 align:middle
that we're there for
the labor market.

34:07.366 --> 34:08.966 align:middle
And it was important
that we send that signal.

34:09.456 --> 34:10.866 align:middle
Fortunately, since then,

34:10.986 --> 34:13.826 align:middle
the labor market has really-and
the unemployment rate have

34:13.826 --> 34:17.066 align:middle
really been moving sideways
at a level that is, you know,

34:17.066 --> 34:20.206 align:middle
well in the range of mainstream
estimates of maximum employment.

34:20.706 --> 34:24.386 align:middle
So that concern has
gotten a lot less.

34:24.866 --> 34:27.686 align:middle
So, you know, you're at
4.2 percent unemployment.

34:27.936 --> 34:29.776 align:middle
I think we were in a
very different situation.

34:29.776 --> 34:31.936 align:middle
And now we have a
situation where, you know,

34:31.936 --> 34:33.546 align:middle
the risks to higher inflation

34:33.546 --> 34:35.996 align:middle
and higher unemployment
have both gone up,

34:35.996 --> 34:38.046 align:middle
as we noted in our
statement, and we've got

34:38.046 --> 34:39.106 align:middle
to monitor both of those.

34:39.106 --> 34:42.236 align:middle
We actually have a
potential situation

34:42.236 --> 34:43.936 align:middle
where there may be a
tradeoff or tension

34:43.936 --> 34:45.166 align:middle
between the two potentially.

34:45.276 --> 34:47.406 align:middle
We don't have it yet,
and we may not have it,

34:47.516 --> 34:48.756 align:middle
but that's what we [may] have,

34:48.756 --> 34:50.116 align:middle
and that's why I think
it's a very different,

34:50.116 --> 34:50.976 align:middle
different situation.

34:51.056 --> 34:51.386 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

34:51.386 --> 34:53.366 align:middle
I, I mean, I guess I want to
follow up by asking how much

34:53.426 --> 34:56.186 align:middle
of a rise in the jobless rate
you would-you could tolerate.

34:56.796 --> 34:57.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:57.316 --> 34:59.756 align:middle
I can't give you a-you
know, a-I'm not going to try

34:59.756 --> 35:00.916 align:middle
to give you a specific number.

35:00.916 --> 35:03.196 align:middle
I'll just say, we've got-we
have to now be looking

35:03.196 --> 35:06.646 align:middle
at both variables and which
of them is, is, you know,

35:07.096 --> 35:09.996 align:middle
demanding-if one of them
is demanding our focus more

35:09.996 --> 35:12.966 align:middle
than the other, that would
tell us what to do with policy.

35:12.966 --> 35:16.256 align:middle
If they're more or less this,
you know, equally distant

35:16.256 --> 35:20.186 align:middle
and equally-or not
distant, then we don't have

35:20.186 --> 35:21.286 align:middle
to make that assessment.

35:21.626 --> 35:23.036 align:middle
You know, the assessment
is, you wait.

35:24.056 --> 35:26.366 align:middle
So I'm not going to try
to be really specific

35:26.366 --> 35:28.696 align:middle
about what we need to see
in terms of the number.

35:28.696 --> 35:33.736 align:middle
But if-look, if we did see, you
know, significant deterioration

35:33.736 --> 35:36.996 align:middle
in the labor market, of course
that's one of our two variables,

35:36.996 --> 35:38.966 align:middle
and we would look to be
able to support that.

35:39.316 --> 35:41.156 align:middle
You'd hope that it wasn't
also coming at a time

35:41.156 --> 35:43.646 align:middle
when inflation was
getting very bad.

35:43.826 --> 35:46.176 align:middle
And, again, we're
speculating here.

35:46.176 --> 35:47.036 align:middle
We don't know this.

35:47.036 --> 35:48.636 align:middle
We don't know any
of these things.

35:48.776 --> 35:50.326 align:middle
It's very hypothetical.

35:51.076 --> 35:51.976 align:middle
We're just going to have to wait

35:51.976 --> 35:53.506 align:middle
and see how the-how
it plays out.

35:54.326 --> 35:55.336 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

35:56.146 --> 35:59.886 align:middle
Thanks. MICHELLE SMITH.

35:59.986 --> 36:00.726 align:middle
Claire. CLAIRE JONES.

36:00.726 --> 36:02.076 align:middle
Claire Jones, Financial Times.

36:02.316 --> 36:06.936 align:middle
In terms of getting some
clarity, we've got some talks

36:06.936 --> 36:10.286 align:middle
at the weekend in Geneva
between the U.S. and China.

36:10.816 --> 36:13.886 align:middle
A lot of economists are
ask-are attaching an awful lot

36:13.886 --> 36:17.246 align:middle
of importance to how-what
we hear from those talks.

36:17.576 --> 36:20.096 align:middle
How much importance are you
attaching to them in terms

36:20.096 --> 36:23.556 align:middle
of judging what will happen to
the U.S. economy going forward?

36:24.016 --> 36:26.516 align:middle
And just in a similar
vein, you know,

36:27.406 --> 36:31.966 align:middle
some economists are saying it's
days, not weeks, that we have

36:31.966 --> 36:35.686 align:middle
until we start to put the U.S.
economy at risk of seeing a sort

36:35.686 --> 36:39.486 align:middle
of pandemic here, with
shortages and higher prices,

36:39.486 --> 36:43.366 align:middle
if we don't kind
of soothe relations

36:43.366 --> 36:44.756 align:middle
between the U.S. and China.

36:44.876 --> 36:46.526 align:middle
So it would be good to have
your view on that, too.

36:46.746 --> 36:47.336 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:47.416 --> 36:49.866 align:middle
So, you know, these are
not talks that we're

36:49.866 --> 36:50.916 align:middle
in any way involved in,

36:50.916 --> 36:52.856 align:middle
so I really can't
comment directly on them.

36:52.856 --> 36:56.246 align:middle
So-but what I will say is
this: You know, we had-coming

36:56.246 --> 36:58.136 align:middle
out of the March meeting, we,

36:58.186 --> 37:00.576 align:middle
we-the public generally
had an assessment

37:00.576 --> 37:01.656 align:middle
of where tariffs were going.

37:01.656 --> 37:03.166 align:middle
And then April 2 happened,

37:03.166 --> 37:06.596 align:middle
and it was really substantially
larger than anticipated

37:06.596 --> 37:09.476 align:middle
in the forecasts that I've
seen and in our forecast.

37:09.986 --> 37:12.846 align:middle
So-and now we-and now we
have a different-we're

37:12.846 --> 37:15.786 align:middle
in a new phase where-it seems
to be we're entering a new phase

37:15.786 --> 37:19.336 align:middle
where the Administration is
entering into beginning talks

37:19.336 --> 37:22.026 align:middle
with a number of our
important trading partners.

37:22.606 --> 37:24.526 align:middle
And that's-that has
the potential

37:24.526 --> 37:27.286 align:middle
to change the picture
materially or not.

37:28.046 --> 37:29.386 align:middle
And so I think it's going

37:29.386 --> 37:31.726 align:middle
to be very important
how that shakes out.

37:31.726 --> 37:33.566 align:middle
But I-you know, we
simply have to wait

37:33.566 --> 37:35.436 align:middle
and see how it works out.

37:35.436 --> 37:38.346 align:middle
It certainly could change the
picture, and we're mindful

37:38.346 --> 37:42.086 align:middle
of not trying to make conclusive
judgments about what will happen

37:42.266 --> 37:45.566 align:middle
at a time when the-you know,
when the facts are changing.

37:46.536 --> 37:46.866 align:middle
CLAIRE JONES.

37:47.206 --> 37:51.566 align:middle
And just on the fall in
shipping volumes from China

37:51.566 --> 37:54.536 align:middle
over these tensions, I mean,
do you share that concern

37:54.646 --> 37:58.296 align:middle
that we could start seeing goods
shortages and higher prices

37:58.296 --> 38:01.346 align:middle
in the coming weeks if this
isn't resolved very quickly?

38:01.676 --> 38:02.256 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:02.526 --> 38:03.136 align:middle
You know, I don't want

38:03.136 --> 38:06.326 align:middle
to get my-we're not-we shouldn't
be involved, even verbally,

38:06.326 --> 38:08.396 align:middle
in questions about the
timing of these things.

38:08.476 --> 38:10.666 align:middle
Yes, we're-of course,
we follow all that data.

38:11.126 --> 38:12.176 align:middle
We see the shipping data.

38:12.176 --> 38:12.866 align:middle
We see all that.

38:12.916 --> 38:16.326 align:middle
But, ultimately, this is for
the Administration to do.

38:16.326 --> 38:20.816 align:middle
This is-you know, this is
their mandate, not ours.

38:20.946 --> 38:23.066 align:middle
And I know they're-as you
can see, they're, again,

38:23.066 --> 38:26.746 align:middle
having-beginning to have
talks with many nations.

38:26.746 --> 38:31.336 align:middle
And that has the potential to
change the picture materially,

38:31.836 --> 38:33.546 align:middle
so we'll just have
to wait and see.

38:35.366 --> 38:36.916 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:37.296 --> 38:40.376 align:middle
Kosuke. KOSUKE TAKAMI.

38:40.616 --> 38:42.006 align:middle
Kosuke Takami with Nikkei.

38:42.076 --> 38:43.456 align:middle
Thank you for doing this.

38:43.626 --> 38:48.236 align:middle
The volume of imported goods
increased significantly

38:48.236 --> 38:49.346 align:middle
in the first quarter.

38:49.856 --> 38:53.706 align:middle
Do you think the decision could
cause a delay in the impact

38:53.706 --> 38:57.666 align:middle
of tariff on inflation,
and does this mean

38:57.946 --> 39:02.226 align:middle
that it will take a longer
time to reduce uncertainty?

39:02.226 --> 39:03.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:03.436 --> 39:04.606 align:middle
The decision we made today?

39:05.546 --> 39:06.216 align:middle
Which decision?

39:06.946 --> 39:07.496 align:middle
KOSUKE TAKAMI.

39:07.496 --> 39:08.986 align:middle
So the future decisions.

39:10.026 --> 39:12.486 align:middle
So the volume of
imports- CHAIR POWELL.

39:12.516 --> 39:13.686 align:middle
Yes. KOSUKE TAKAMI.

39:13.686 --> 39:17.276 align:middle
-imported goods has
increased significantly.

39:17.276 --> 39:24.386 align:middle
So the impact of the
imported inflation may delay.

39:24.506 --> 39:29.936 align:middle
So, what is the impact
to your future decision?

39:29.996 --> 39:30.356 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:30.356 --> 39:34.446 align:middle
Okay, so, I mean, I
think we-I think we think

39:34.446 --> 39:38.066 align:middle
that the-you know,
there was a big spike

39:38.066 --> 39:40.926 align:middle
in imports-right?-very
big, historically large,

39:40.926 --> 39:43.556 align:middle
really, and-to beat tariffs.

39:43.556 --> 39:46.876 align:middle
And now that should actually
reverse so that it's-you know,

39:46.876 --> 39:50.086 align:middle
it's the difference between-it's
exports minus imports,

39:50.086 --> 39:52.536 align:middle
so-and imports were huge,

39:52.536 --> 39:55.656 align:middle
and so then it conveyed a
very negative contribution

39:55.656 --> 39:58.976 align:middle
to U.S. GDP-annualized GDP

39:58.976 --> 40:00.616 align:middle
in the first quarter,
as we all know.

40:01.216 --> 40:03.076 align:middle
So that could, in
the second quarter,

40:03.686 --> 40:06.546 align:middle
be reversed so that
we have, you know,

40:07.176 --> 40:10.606 align:middle
an unusually large contribution
to-unusually positive.

40:10.606 --> 40:13.906 align:middle
That's very likely as
imports drop sharply.

40:14.396 --> 40:15.866 align:middle
You could also have-you know,

40:15.866 --> 40:19.066 align:middle
very likely you'll
have restatements

40:19.066 --> 40:21.156 align:middle
of the-of the first quarter.

40:21.596 --> 40:24.076 align:middle
It'll turn out that
consumer spending was higher.

40:24.076 --> 40:25.826 align:middle
It will turn out that
inventories were higher.

40:25.826 --> 40:28.496 align:middle
And so you'll see-you'll
see those data revised up.

40:29.176 --> 40:31.036 align:middle
It may actually go into
the third quarter, too.

40:31.196 --> 40:33.886 align:middle
And so I think it's going-this
whole process is going to,

40:34.546 --> 40:37.096 align:middle
a little bit, make it harder

40:37.096 --> 40:39.456 align:middle
to make a clean assessment
of U.S. demand.

40:39.796 --> 40:42.916 align:middle
I mentioned private
domestic final purchases,

40:42.916 --> 40:44.776 align:middle
which doesn't have
inventories, government,

40:45.456 --> 40:49.696 align:middle
or-inventories, government.

40:50.586 --> 40:56.276 align:middle
Anyway, it's a cleaner
read on private demand.

40:56.476 --> 40:59.636 align:middle
But that, that, too, probably
was flattered a little bit

40:59.806 --> 41:04.466 align:middle
by-you know, by strong demand
for imports to be tariffed.

41:04.466 --> 41:06.136 align:middle
So that might overstate.

41:06.296 --> 41:07.306 align:middle
It's a really good reading,

41:07.306 --> 41:09.176 align:middle
3 percent PDFP in
the first quarter.

41:09.176 --> 41:10.166 align:middle
That might actually overstate.

41:10.166 --> 41:12.086 align:middle
So it's not really going
to-I don't think it's going

41:12.086 --> 41:13.096 align:middle
to affect our decisions.

41:13.456 --> 41:15.676 align:middle
I will just say, though,
that it's a little confusing

41:15.676 --> 41:19.096 align:middle
and it's probably less
confusing to us than it would be

41:19.276 --> 41:21.726 align:middle
to the general public as
we try to explain this.

41:21.726 --> 41:24.356 align:middle
You know, it's complicated,
and, you know,

41:24.556 --> 41:25.916 align:middle
GDP is sending a signal.

41:25.916 --> 41:27.466 align:middle
PDFP is sending a signal.

41:27.466 --> 41:29.646 align:middle
It's a little bit confusing.

41:29.646 --> 41:31.906 align:middle
But I think we understand what's
going, and it's not really going

41:31.906 --> 41:32.776 align:middle
to change things for us.

41:32.776 --> 41:34.386 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:34.386 --> 41:34.656 align:middle
Courtenay.

41:37.926 --> 41:38.586 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

41:38.586 --> 41:39.066 align:middle
Thank you.

41:39.676 --> 41:40.686 align:middle
Courtenay Brown from Axios.

41:40.686 --> 41:43.506 align:middle
I guess, you know, we talked
about some of the indications

41:43.506 --> 41:46.666 align:middle
of potential layoffs,
price hikes,

41:46.666 --> 41:50.116 align:middle
an economic slowdown all being
evident in the soft data.

41:50.256 --> 41:54.796 align:middle
I'm curious why the Fed needs
to wait for that to translate

41:55.176 --> 41:59.286 align:middle
into hard data to, you
know, make any type

41:59.286 --> 42:01.236 align:middle
of monetary policy decision,

42:01.276 --> 42:06.086 align:middle
especially if the hard data is
not as timely or might be warped

42:06.166 --> 42:09.266 align:middle
by tariff-related effects.

42:09.336 --> 42:11.906 align:middle
Are you worried that the
soft data might be some sort

42:11.906 --> 42:13.556 align:middle
of false warning?

42:14.546 --> 42:15.036 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:15.036 --> 42:18.416 align:middle
No. I mean, it's-look, the-look
at the state of the economies.

42:18.656 --> 42:19.796 align:middle
The labor market is solid.

42:19.796 --> 42:20.916 align:middle
Inflation is low.

42:21.416 --> 42:23.396 align:middle
We can afford to be
patient as things unfold.

42:23.396 --> 42:26.626 align:middle
There's no real cost to
our waiting at this point.

42:26.826 --> 42:28.606 align:middle
Also, the sense of
it-the sense of it is,

42:29.176 --> 42:31.156 align:middle
we're not sure what the
right thing will be.

42:31.206 --> 42:33.996 align:middle
You know, there should be
some increase in inflation.

42:33.996 --> 42:35.806 align:middle
There should be some
increase in unemployment.

42:35.806 --> 42:37.336 align:middle
Those call for different
responses.

42:37.906 --> 42:40.276 align:middle
And so until we know-potentially
call

42:40.276 --> 42:45.236 align:middle
for different responses-and so,
you know, until we know more,

42:45.456 --> 42:48.046 align:middle
we have the ability
to wait and see.

42:48.046 --> 42:50.176 align:middle
And it seems to be a
pretty clear decision.

42:50.466 --> 42:52.196 align:middle
Everyone on the Committee
supported waiting.

42:52.776 --> 42:55.336 align:middle
And so that's why we're waiting.

42:56.216 --> 42:57.086 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

42:57.086 --> 43:00.836 align:middle
Just a very quick
follow-up: There was this sort

43:00.836 --> 43:04.616 align:middle
of vibe session, if you will,
where the sentiments expressed

43:04.616 --> 43:08.536 align:middle
in soft data did not translate
into the hard economic data.

43:09.096 --> 43:11.396 align:middle
Are you-how are you
thinking about that

43:11.626 --> 43:14.926 align:middle
when interpreting some of the
signs in the softer survey data?

43:14.926 --> 43:15.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:15.796 --> 43:19.506 align:middle
You know, I think, going
back a number of years,

43:19.866 --> 43:22.306 align:middle
the link between sentiment data

43:22.836 --> 43:24.876 align:middle
and consumer spending
has been weak.

43:25.246 --> 43:26.956 align:middle
It's not been a strong
link at all.

43:27.726 --> 43:30.786 align:middle
On the other hand, we
haven't had a move of this,

43:31.196 --> 43:33.206 align:middle
you know, speed and size.

43:33.696 --> 43:37.026 align:middle
So it wouldn't be the case
that we're looking at this

43:37.026 --> 43:38.406 align:middle
and just completely
dismissing it.

43:38.826 --> 43:42.936 align:middle
But it's another
reason to wait and see.

43:42.936 --> 43:44.546 align:middle
You're right that
we had a couple

43:44.546 --> 43:45.606 align:middle
of years during the pandemic

43:45.636 --> 43:50.636 align:middle
where people were saying-just
very downbeat surveys and going

43:50.636 --> 43:51.436 align:middle
out and spending money.

43:51.986 --> 43:55.346 align:middle
So that can happen, and that
may happen to some degree here.

43:55.346 --> 43:55.936 align:middle
We just don't know.

43:55.936 --> 43:59.346 align:middle
This is an outsized change
in sentiment, though,

43:59.346 --> 44:02.626 align:middle
and so none of us are
looking at this and saying

44:02.626 --> 44:04.606 align:middle
that we're sure one
way or the other.

44:05.056 --> 44:05.366 align:middle
We're not.

44:05.766 --> 44:06.136 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

44:06.686 --> 44:09.556 align:middle
Matt. MATT EGAN.

44:10.806 --> 44:11.506 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

44:11.506 --> 44:13.046 align:middle
Matt Egan with CNN.

44:13.046 --> 44:14.066 align:middle
So you mentioned earlier

44:14.066 --> 44:16.656 align:middle
that you're monitoring
the shipping data.

44:17.106 --> 44:20.126 align:middle
And we have seen in the shipping
data that imports from China

44:20.266 --> 44:22.966 align:middle
into the Port of Los
Angeles have plunged.

44:23.156 --> 44:26.426 align:middle
And that has raised concerns
about potential shortages.

44:26.966 --> 44:32.146 align:middle
What tools, if any, does the
Fed have to ensure that prices

44:32.206 --> 44:34.926 align:middle
and inflation expectations
don't get out of hand

44:35.326 --> 44:39.266 align:middle
if tariffs do cause significant
supply chain disruptions?

44:40.746 --> 44:41.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:41.266 --> 44:45.086 align:middle
I mean, we don't have, you know,
the kind of tools that are good

44:45.086 --> 44:46.876 align:middle
at dealing with supply
chain problems.

44:46.916 --> 44:47.886 align:middle
We don't have that at all.

44:48.076 --> 44:50.156 align:middle
That's a-that's a job
for the Administration

44:50.156 --> 44:51.856 align:middle
and for the private
sector more than anything.

44:52.596 --> 44:56.076 align:middle
You know, what we can do with
our interest rate tool is,

44:56.076 --> 44:58.816 align:middle
we can support-be more or
less supportive of demand.

44:59.176 --> 45:01.376 align:middle
And that's-that'd be a
very inefficient way to try

45:01.376 --> 45:02.766 align:middle
to fix supply chain problems.

45:03.566 --> 45:07.356 align:middle
But, you know, we don't see-we
don't see the inflation yet.

45:07.446 --> 45:09.306 align:middle
We're, of course,
reading the same stories

45:09.306 --> 45:11.156 align:middle
and watching the same
data as everybody else.

45:11.856 --> 45:16.266 align:middle
And, you know, right now,
we see inflation, you know,

45:16.266 --> 45:18.236 align:middle
kind of moving sideways
at a fairly low level.

45:18.636 --> 45:18.886 align:middle
MATT EGAN.

45:18.936 --> 45:21.426 align:middle
If I could follow up on that,
President Trump has indicated

45:21.426 --> 45:24.406 align:middle
that he will likely name
a replacement for you

45:24.406 --> 45:27.086 align:middle
when your term as Chair
expires next year.

45:27.176 --> 45:29.626 align:middle
But your position
on the Board runs

45:29.626 --> 45:31.896 align:middle
through January 2028, I believe.

45:32.076 --> 45:35.606 align:middle
Would you consider remaining
on the Fed Board even

45:35.606 --> 45:36.736 align:middle
if you're no longer Chair?

45:36.856 --> 45:37.246 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:37.316 --> 45:38.986 align:middle
So I don't have anything
for you on that.

45:39.056 --> 45:43.586 align:middle
My whole focus is on-and my
colleagues' focus is all on,

45:44.136 --> 45:48.026 align:middle
you know, trying to navigate
this tricky passage we're

45:48.026 --> 45:50.496 align:middle
in right now, trying to
make the right decisions.

45:50.496 --> 45:52.236 align:middle
You know, we want to
make the best decisions

45:52.516 --> 45:53.736 align:middle
for the people that we serve.

45:53.856 --> 45:55.726 align:middle
That's what we think
about day and night.

45:55.846 --> 45:58.316 align:middle
And this is a challenging
situation, and that's,

45:58.356 --> 45:59.876 align:middle
that's 100 percent of
our focus right now.

46:01.136 --> 46:01.576 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:01.666 --> 46:04.956 align:middle
Let's go to Jennifer
for the last question.

46:05.266 --> 46:06.066 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

46:06.066 --> 46:07.986 align:middle
Thank you so much, Chair Powell.

46:07.986 --> 46:09.176 align:middle
Jennifer Schonberger
with Yahoo Finance.

46:09.176 --> 46:10.626 align:middle
Public records of your schedule

46:10.626 --> 46:12.696 align:middle
so far this year show no
meetings with President Trump.

46:13.056 --> 46:14.816 align:middle
Past Presidents Obama, Bush,

46:14.816 --> 46:16.676 align:middle
and Clinton have all
met with Fed Chairs.

46:16.906 --> 46:19.786 align:middle
And you met with Trump
during his first term.

46:19.886 --> 46:22.286 align:middle
Why haven't you asked for a
meeting yet with the President?

46:22.916 --> 46:23.226 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:23.416 --> 46:24.646 align:middle
I've never asked for a meeting

46:24.646 --> 46:26.436 align:middle
with any President,
and I never will.

46:26.736 --> 46:28.476 align:middle
It's not-I wouldn't do that.

46:28.476 --> 46:30.536 align:middle
There's never a reason for
me to ask for a meeting.

46:30.536 --> 46:31.676 align:middle
It's always been the other way.

46:31.676 --> 46:32.296 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

46:32.296 --> 46:33.776 align:middle
So would you want
to meet with him

46:33.776 --> 46:35.226 align:middle
if given the opportunity-
CHAIR POWELL.

46:35.226 --> 46:36.206 align:middle
I never- JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

46:36.206 --> 46:37.606 align:middle
-to get more information?

46:37.606 --> 46:37.916 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:37.916 --> 46:40.356 align:middle
I never-it's never an
initiative that I take.

46:40.416 --> 46:42.076 align:middle
It's always an initiative-I-you
know, I don't think it's

46:42.076 --> 46:43.806 align:middle
up to a Fed Chair to seek a
meeting with the President,

46:43.806 --> 46:45.066 align:middle
although maybe some
have done so.

46:45.066 --> 46:48.176 align:middle
I've never done so, and I can't
imagine myself doing that.

46:48.176 --> 46:50.986 align:middle
It's-I think it's
always-comes the other way:

46:50.986 --> 46:52.486 align:middle
A President wants
to meet with you.

46:52.546 --> 46:53.516 align:middle
But that hasn't happened.

46:53.516 --> 46:53.916 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

46:53.916 --> 46:56.016 align:middle
And if I could just ask one
question on monetary policy,

46:56.266 --> 46:59.446 align:middle
when it is time to cut rates,
how will you determine how far

46:59.506 --> 47:02.326 align:middle
down rates will have to come
to try to keep a balance

47:02.566 --> 47:05.376 align:middle
on the inflation mandate
as employment weakens?

47:06.226 --> 47:07.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:08.236 --> 47:10.756 align:middle
You know, I think once you have
a direction-a clear direction,

47:10.756 --> 47:13.336 align:middle
you can make a-we can make a
judgment about how fast to move

47:13.336 --> 47:13.936 align:middle
and that kind of thing.

47:14.356 --> 47:18.556 align:middle
So it's-really, the harder
question is the timing, I think,

47:18.556 --> 47:20.196 align:middle
and when will that become clear?

47:20.886 --> 47:24.446 align:middle
And, fortunately, as I
mentioned, we have our policy

47:24.446 --> 47:26.476 align:middle
in a good place, the
economy's in a good place,

47:27.086 --> 47:32.196 align:middle
and it's really appropriate,
we think, for us to be patient

47:32.196 --> 47:35.426 align:middle
and wait for things to
unfold as we get more clarity

47:36.126 --> 47:43.506 align:middle
about what we should do.

47:43.596 --> 47:47.996 align:middle
Thanks very much.

